REPRODUCER (perl 3 / 4, base and s543b tree 0 / 0):
    use IO::File; my $f = "/tmp/x.$$";
    my $io = IO::File->new($f, "w"); print $io "io\n"; undef $io; print "size4: ", -s $f, "\n";
    $io = IO::File->new($f, "w"); $io->print("io2\n"); $io = undef; print "size5: ", -s $f, "\n";

WHY (read, not yet measured in the runtime): `p-flush-dropped-handle` (cl/pcl-runtime.lisp, s543b) peeks the variable`s slot and flushes when the value is an `sb-sys:fd-stream`; an IO::File object is a blessed GLOB handle (docs/not-supported.md ns-escaping-filehandle: "a GLOB handle (Symbol::gensym, IO::File->new, ...) is named in the handle table, so it stays open until exit"), so the type test says no.  THE CHEAP DISCRIMINATING MEASUREMENT: print the type of `(p-box-value $io)` after `IO::File->new` -- if it is a typeglob / a box whose IO slot holds the fd-stream, the flush needs ONE more arm (the glob`s IO slot), still a flush and never a close.

NOT this task: the scope-exit shapes (`{ my $in = IO::File->new(...); print $in ...; }` then `-s`, perl 7 / PCL 0; a sub that returns without closing) -- those are the documented MOMENT limit of ns-escaping-filehandle (#2006 / #2370).  This task is only the statement the program WRITES to say it is done with the handle.

GUARD when fixed: Pl/t/fh-collect-01.t beside s543b`s row 7 (the IO::File spelling, both statements; inverse fails on the s543b tree).

